---
// HiawathaExplainer.astro
// Rewritten Hiawatha narrative — witty New Yorker editorial tone
// Covers Longfellow's 1855 publication, the Haudenosaunee/Ojibwe conflation,
// the 1931 National Forest naming, and MBTN's role organizing the ride.
import historicalPhotos from '../../public/data/historical-photos.json';
import ShieldMotif from './ShieldMotif.astro';
---

<section data-reveal id="history" class="hiawatha-section py-[--spacing-block]">
  <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4">
    <h2 class="py-10 text-3xl mb-[--spacing-element]">The Poem, the Forest, the Ride</h2>

    <div class="editorial-grid">

      <!-- VIS-04: Poem sub-section wrapper with independent parallax background -->
      <div class="subsection-bg poem-section" data-bg-fade>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-display text-amber-500 mt-0 mb-4 tracking-wide section-heading">The Poem</h3>

        <p class="drop-cap">
          In 1855, Henry Wadsworth Longfellow committed one of American literature's
          most spectacular acts of geographic and cultural confusion. He published
          <em>The Song of Hiawatha</em>, an epic poem in the meter of the Finnish
          <em>Kalevala</em> (drawing heavily on Ojibwe oral traditions that ethnographer
          Henry Rowe Schoolcraft had collected along the shores of Lake Superior).
          Longfellow set the whole thing in Michigan's Upper Peninsula, peopled it with
          Ojibwe figures and Ojibwe landscape, and then — for reasons that remain a
          testament to the perils of desk-bound scholarship — named his hero after
          someone else entirely.
        </p>

                {historicalPhotos.length > 0 && (
          <figure class="museum-plate breakout">
            <img
              src={historicalPhotos[0].thumb}
              alt={`${historicalPhotos[0].title} by ${historicalPhotos[0].artist}, ${historicalPhotos[0].year}`}
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
            <figcaption>
              <em>{historicalPhotos[0].title}</em> — {historicalPhotos[0].artist}, {historicalPhotos[0].year}. {historicalPhotos[0].source}
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        )}

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-display text-turquoise-400 section-heading section-heading--gap mb-4 tracking-wide">The Confusion</h3>

        <p class="drop-cap">
          Hiawatha was a real historical figure, but he had nothing to do with the
          Ojibwe, Lake Superior, or Michigan. He was a Mohawk chief — a
          <em>Haudenosaunee</em> (Iroquois) statesman from present-day southern Ontario
          and upper New York who, alongside the prophet Deganawida, helped unite the
          Five Nations (Seneca, Cayuga, Onondaga, Oneida, and Kanyen'kehà:ka) into
          one of the most sophisticated political confederacies in the precolonial world.
          As the primary carrier of Deganawida's message of peace, Hiawatha was an
          agent of diplomacy and reconciliation, subordinating himself to former enemies
          in the name of unity. He was, in other words, precisely the kind of figure
          you would not confuse with Nanabozho: the shape-shifting, endlessly scheming
          Ojibwe trickster-hero who <em>actually</em> lived in Schoolcraft's stories.
        </p>

        <p>
          Longfellow apparently found the names interchangeable. He took Nanabozho's
          adventures, Nanabozho's landscape, Nanabozho's Ojibwe world — and published
          the whole thing under Hiawatha's name. Scholars have spent the better part of
          two centuries untangling the wreckage. As one assessment put it, the poem
          ultimately became
        </p>

        <blockquote class="pull-quote breakout">
          <p>"A romanticized conflation of disparate Indian tribes, their traditions, and
          their legends" — one that presented Native American culture as doomed, a relic
          of the past inevitably making way for Euro-American expansion.</p>
          <footer class="pull-quote-attribution">
            — <cite><a href="https://www.american.edu/arts/hiawatha-in-context.cfm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">American University, "Hiawatha in Context"</a></cite>
          </footer>
          <ShieldMotif size={12} class="pull-quote-ornament" />
        </blockquote>

      </div>

      <!-- VIS-04: Forest sub-section wrapper with independent parallax background -->
      <div class="subsection-bg forest-section" data-bg-fade>

        <div class="two-col-moment">
          <div class="two-col-prose">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-display text-sun-400 section-heading section-heading--gap mb-4 tracking-wide">The Forest</h3>

            <p class="drop-cap">
              None of this diminished the poem's popularity. <em>The Song of Hiawatha</em>
              became one of the best-selling poetry collections of the nineteenth century,
              inspiring parlor readings, theatrical productions, and, in 1931, the naming of
              nearly 900,000 acres of Upper Peninsula forest. The federal government
              designated this land the <strong>Hiawatha National Forest</strong> — after
              Longfellow's fictional protagonist, not after either of the actual historical
              figures whose identities had been quietly merged to create him. The forest
              spreads today from Lake Superior to Lake Michigan, sheltering over a hundred
              miles of Great Lakes shoreline, a wilderness of hardwoods and cedar swamp and
              glacial lake that has changed little since Schoolcraft paddled through taking
              notes.
            </p>
          </div>

          {historicalPhotos.length > 1 && (
            <figure class="museum-plate">
              <img
                src={historicalPhotos[1].thumb}
                alt={`${historicalPhotos[1].title} by ${historicalPhotos[1].artist}, ${historicalPhotos[1].year}`}
                loading="lazy"
                decoding="async"
              />
              <figcaption>
                <em>{historicalPhotos[1].title}</em> — {historicalPhotos[1].artist}, {historicalPhotos[1].year}. {historicalPhotos[1].source}
              </figcaption>
            </figure>
          )}
        </div>

      </div>

      <!-- VIS-04: Ride sub-section wrapper with independent parallax background -->
      <div class="subsection-bg ride-section" data-bg-fade>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-display text-scarlet-400 section-heading mb-4 tracking-wide">The Ride</h3>

        <p class="drop-cap">
          Into this magnificent, improbably-named landscape, the
          <a href="https://mbtn.org" class="underline hover:text-amber-300">Munising Bay Trail Network</a>
          (MBTN) — a 501(c)(3) nonprofit devoted to building and maintaining singletrack
          trails across the region. <em>Hiawatha's Revenge</em> starts and finishes in
          Munising, threading quiet forest roads, rugged two-track, and remote
          landscape through exactly the terrain Longfellow imagined without ever
          visiting. It is a ride that tests your legs, rewards your soul, and, for those
          who appreciate a good literary irony, puts you directly inside one of the
          nineteenth century's most celebrated mistakes.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .hiawatha-section {
    background-color: var(--color-forest-950);
    position: relative;
  }

  /* ============================================================
     Magazine Editorial Grid
     Named tracks: prose (65ch centered), breakout (wider), full (edge-to-edge)
     ============================================================ */
  .editorial-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns:
      [full-start] 1fr
      [breakout-start] 1fr
      [prose-start] min(65ch, 100%) [prose-end]
      1fr [breakout-end]
      1fr [full-end];
    row-gap: 0;
  }

  /* Default: all children in prose column */
  .editorial-grid > * {
    grid-column: prose;
  }

  /* Breakout elements span wider than prose */
  .editorial-grid > .breakout {
    grid-column: breakout;
  }

  /* VIS-04: Sub-section wrappers span full editorial grid width and replicate grid tracks */
  .subsection-bg {
    grid-column: full;
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns:
      [full-start] 1fr
      [breakout-start] 1fr
      [prose-start] min(65ch, 100%) [prose-end]
      1fr [breakout-end]
      1fr [full-end];
    row-gap: 0;
  }

  /* Default: children in prose column (mirrors .editorial-grid > *) */
  .subsection-bg > * {
    grid-column: prose;
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
  }

  .subsection-bg > .breakout {
    grid-column: breakout;
  }

  .subsection-bg > .two-col-moment {
    grid-column: breakout;
  }

  /* Sub-section padding to maintain breathing room between sections */
  .poem-section {
    padding-top: 0;
    padding-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .forest-section {
    padding-top: 2rem;
    padding-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .ride-section {
    padding-top: 2rem;
    padding-bottom: 0;
  }

  /* ============================================================
     VIS-04: ::before background image system — Remington paintings
     Dark-mode default (always present). Reduced-motion block below overrides.
     ============================================================ */
  .subsection-bg::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: center;
    opacity: 0;
    transition: opacity 0.6s ease;
    pointer-events: none;
    z-index: 0;
    filter: sepia(80%) saturate(20%) brightness(0.6);
  }

  .subsection-bg.bg-visible::before {
    opacity: 0.08;
  }

  .poem-section::before {
    background-image: url('/thumbs/historical/remington-hiawatha-departure-1891.webp');
  }

  .forest-section::before {
    background-image: url('/thumbs/historical/remington-hiawatha-fasting-1891.webp');
  }

  .ride-section::before {
    background-image: url('/thumbs/historical/remington-hiawatha-departure-1891.webp');
  }

  /* prefers-reduced-motion: static backgrounds, no transition */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .subsection-bg::before {
      transition: none;
      opacity: 0.04;
    }
  }

  /* ============================================================
     Section Headings
     ============================================================ */
  .section-heading {
    /* inherits grid-column: prose from .subsection-bg > * */
  }

  .section-heading--gap {
    margin-top: 5rem;
  }

  /* ============================================================
     Paragraph Spacing
     ============================================================ */
  .editorial-grid p {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    color: var(--color-cream-100);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  /* ============================================================
     Museum Plate Image Treatment
     ============================================================ */
  .museum-plate {
    background: var(--color-cream-200);
    padding: 1.25rem;
    border-radius: 0.25rem;
    box-shadow: 0 0 0px 2px var(--color-amber-300), 0 0 0 12px var(--color-cream-200), 0 0 0 16px var(--color-amber-300);
    max-width: 560px;
    margin-top: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .museum-plate img {
    width: 100%;
    display: block;
    filter: sepia(80%) saturate(30%) brightness(0.9);
    border: 1px solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
  }

  .museum-plate figcaption {
    color: var(--color-forest-900);
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    text-align: center;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  /* Standalone breakout museum-plate: centered within breakout track */
  .subsection-bg > .museum-plate.breakout {
    margin-left: auto;
    margin-right: auto;
  }

  /* ============================================================
     Two-column moment: "The Forest" section at >=900px
     ============================================================ */
  .two-col-moment {
    grid-column: breakout;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 2rem;
    align-items: start;
  }

  @media (max-width: 899px) {
    .two-col-moment {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }

  /* ============================================================
     Pull Quote
     ============================================================ */
  .pull-quote {
    background: var(--color-forest-800);
    border-left: 4px solid var(--color-gold-500);
    border-top: none;
    border-right: none;
    border-bottom: none;
    padding: 1.5rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
    border-radius: 0.25rem;
    position: relative;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-style: normal;
    line-height: 1.6;
    width: 100%;
  }

  .pull-quote p {
    font-size: var(--font-size-2xl);
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--color-cream-100);
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .pull-quote-attribution {
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
    color: var(--color-cream-200);
  }

  .pull-quote-attribution cite a {
    color: var(--color-cream-200);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .pull-quote-ornament {
    color: var(--color-gold-500);
    opacity: 0.4;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    display: block;
  }

  /* ============================================================
     Drop Caps — subsection first paragraphs and pull quote
     ============================================================ */
  .drop-cap::first-letter {
    font-family: var(--font-garamond, 'EB Garamond', serif);
    font-size: 4.5rem;
    float: left;
    line-height: 0.8;
    padding-right: 0.5rem;
    padding-top: 0.15rem;
    color: var(--color-gold-500);
    text-shadow: 2px 3px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3);
  }

  .pull-quote p::first-letter {
    font-family: var(--font-garamond, 'EB Garamond', serif);
    font-size: 4.5rem;
    float: left;
    line-height: 0.8;
    padding-right: 0.5rem;
    padding-top: 0.15rem;
    color: var(--color-gold-500);
    text-shadow: 2px 3px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3);
  }

  /* ============================================================
     Responsive adjustments
     ============================================================ */

  /* Below 900px: grid tracks collapse, breakout = prose = full width */
  @media (max-width: 899px) {
    .editorial-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .editorial-grid > * {
      grid-column: 1;
    }

    .editorial-grid > .breakout {
      grid-column: 1;
    }

    .subsection-bg {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .subsection-bg > * {
      grid-column: 1;
    }

    .subsection-bg > .breakout {
      grid-column: 1;
    }

    .subsection-bg > .two-col-moment {
      grid-column: 1;
    }

    .two-col-moment {
      grid-column: 1;
    }

    .museum-plate {
      max-width: 100%;
    }
  }

  /* Below 640px: pull quote padding reduces, museum-plate constrained */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .pull-quote {
      padding: 1rem;
    }

    .pull-quote p {
      font-size: var(--font-size-xl);
    }

    .museum-plate {
      max-width: 100%;
    }
  }

  /* ============================================================
     Light-mode overrides — scoped to .hiawatha-section to avoid leaking
     MUST be last block so it overrides all default (dark-mode) rules above
     at equal specificity via source order.
     ============================================================ */
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: light) {
    .hiawatha-section {
      background-color: var(--color-cream-100);
    }

    .hiawatha-section h2 {
      color: var(--color-forest-900);
      text-shadow: none;
    }

    .hiawatha-section h3 {
      text-shadow: none;
    }

    .hiawatha-section .text-amber-500 {
      color: var(--color-rust-600);
    }

    .hiawatha-section .text-turquoise-400 {
      color: var(--color-turquoise-700);
    }

    .hiawatha-section .text-sun-400 {
      color: var(--color-rust-600);
    }

    .hiawatha-section .text-scarlet-400 {
      color: var(--color-scarlet-700);
    }

    .editorial-grid p {
      color: var(--color-forest-900);
    }

    .hiawatha-section a {
      color: var(--color-forest-700);
    }

    .hiawatha-section a:hover {
      color: var(--color-rust-600);
    }

    .pull-quote {
      background: var(--color-cream-200);
      border-left-color: var(--color-rust-600);
    }

    .pull-quote p {
      color: var(--color-forest-900);
    }

    .pull-quote-attribution {
      color: var(--color-forest-800);
    }

    .pull-quote-attribution cite a {
      color: var(--color-forest-800);
    }

    .drop-cap::first-letter,
    .pull-quote p::first-letter {
      color: var(--color-rust-600);
      text-shadow: none;
    }

    .subsection-bg::before {
      filter: sepia(80%) saturate(15%) brightness(1.2);
    }

    .subsection-bg.bg-visible::before {
      opacity: 0.12;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // VIS-04: Independent background fade per sub-section
  if (!window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) {
    const sections = document.querySelectorAll('[data-bg-fade]');
    if (sections.length > 0) {
      const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
        entries.forEach(entry => {
          entry.target.classList.toggle('bg-visible', entry.isIntersecting);
        });
      }, { threshold: 0.15 });
      sections.forEach(section => observer.observe(section));
    }
  }
</script>
